# V7b batched decode investigation, 2026-10-18

Machine and model as in 06-server-scheduling.log (RTX 5050 Laptop, 40 W cap,
Qwen3.5-4B Q8 CUDA INT8, `--ctx 2048`). Accepted baseline: the V7 scheduler
(`marin-v7d` = current tree before this milestone).

## Profile of real decode (before any change)

`marin-run bench --profile`, prompt 128, one-token passes: 23.9 ms/token,
weight bandwidth 173 GB/s. Per token: mlp 8.98 ms (260 GB/s), qkv projections
4.33 (120 GB/s), DeltaNet recurrence 2.92, lm head 2.12, attention out-proj
1.72, norm 0.90, state transaction copy 0.81, attention 0.44, other ~1.5.
531 kernel launches and 72 allocations per pass. Matmul stages are 66% of the
step and are weight-read bound; recurrence/attention/state handling is ~20%.

Multi-token proxy (single sequence, `crates/model/tests/zz_probe.rs`, mean of 8
rounds, context 128, existing kernels): a pass of N tokens costs
1: 21.2, 2: 35.2, 3: 42.7, 4: 50.1 ms. mlp 7.96 -> 14.97 -> 18.40 -> 22.39 ms:
the existing N-token Q8 kernel (one weight row per warp, no load unrolling)
does NOT share the weight read; N tokens cost ~N single-token passes minus 25%.
So batching with the existing kernels is capped at 1.20x (N=2), 1.49x (N=3).

Same proxy after adding an N-token variant of the optimized 4-rows-per-warp,
unrolled one-token matvec (N = 2..4, decoded weight reused for every token):
1: 20.2, 2: 22.4, 3: 26.2, 4: 29.6 ms; mlp 7.62 / 7.75 / 8.42 / 9.49 ms.
Aggregate decode ceiling: 1.80x (N=2), 2.31x (N=3), 2.73x (N=4) before the
extra per-sequence copies and state staging a real batch pays.

Diagnosis: the limit is weight reads in the multi-row kernel plus launches,
not GEMM compute. The recurrence (2 ms/token) and attention are per-sequence
and do not amortize.

## Gates, fixed BEFORE implementation

Correctness (all required):
- B1 A batch of 2 and of 3 independent sequences at different positions gives
  logits bit-identical to separate single-token passes (greedy choice and
  every logit), and leaves each cache so that following passes also match.
- B2 Failure atomicity: a batch that fails (for example one cache full) leaves
  every cache unchanged. Cancellation, rollback (truncate/replay) and session
  checkpoints behave as in V7: pinned HF verify unchanged (exact token, max
  logit 1.31e-5, relative RMS 3.288e-6), greedy text of every client equal to
  the V7 scheduler's.
- B3 Existing backends (Qwen3, Granite, CPU) and the planner are untouched.

Performance (all required; V7 scheduler is the comparison, same conditions):
- P1 Aggregate decode events/s with 2 and 3 concurrent decoders at least
  1.30x and 1.50x the V7 scheduler (V7: ~37 events/s per stream, no gain).
- P2 three_interactive: TTFT p95 <= 1.19 s (V7 1.08 +10%); mean completion
  not worse than V7's 1.86 s. long_plus_two: interactive TTFT p95 <= 2.0 s,
  long job total <= 15.0 s.
- P3 Solo short TTFT and solo long total within +3% of V7 (0.340 s, 12.35 s).
- P4 Peak process VRAM growth <= 128 MiB vs V7 at B=3; RSS growth < 128 MiB
  over three runs; device free VRAM at peak >= 512 MiB.

Short-only scheduling overhead (separate, small change): order decode steps by
request age (oldest first) and let a new request's prompt pass go before
decode steps. Gate: three_interactive mean completion <= 1.65 s with TTFT p95
<= 1.10 s, all V7 gates kept. Rejected unless it passes.

Decision rule: if the server-side integration of batching requires an
architectural rewrite (an engine thread that owns all caches, a new
generation loop), stop and report the measured ceiling instead of building it.

## Implementation (kept)

- `quant.cu`: N-token (N = 2..4) variant of the 4-rows-per-warp, unrolled
  one-token Q8 matvec; per-token accumulation order is the one-token kernel's.
  Used only by the precise path (Qwen3.5); Qwen3 and Granite paths unchanged.
- `Qwen35Gpu::forward_batch`: one decode step for up to 4 sequences. Norms,
  projections, MLP and the LM head run over one row per sequence; convolution,
  recurrence, RoPE position, KV write and attention run per sequence on that
  sequence's own state through single-row scratch (row copies). Every cache is
  staged first and committed together, so a failed batch changes nothing.
  `pass_chunk` is shared with the single-sequence path (`Rows::One/Batch`).
- `Engine::decode_batch`: lends caches by value, falls back to one pass per
  sequence if the batch fails. `Gate::decode`: the decode step at the front of
  the queue takes every waiting decode step (up to 3) and runs them as one
  pass; it waits at most 4 ms for decoders that stepped in the last 100 ms.
  Caches are lent through the gate by value (a vacant stand-in is left behind)
  so no `unsafe` or shared mutable state is needed.
- Bug fixed on the way: the leader must mark the gate running before the
  collection wait, or a second leader takes its step ("returned no result").

## Engine-level batch (real `forward_batch` vs one `forward` per sequence)

Context 128+, mean of 40 steps (`07-batch-probe.rs.txt`):

| B | batched step | sequential steps | aggregate gain |
|--:|---:|---:|---:|
| 1 | 25.16 ms | 25.05 ms | 1.00x |
| 2 | 28.75 ms (69.6 tok/s) | 49.64 ms | 1.73x |
| 3 | 34.20 ms (87.7 tok/s) | 74.72 ms | 2.18x |
| 4 | 38.67 ms (103.4 tok/s) | 99.12 ms | 2.56x |

Logits are bit-identical to the single-sequence passes for B = 2, 3, 4 at
different positions over 12 steps on the real model
(`crates/model/tests/qwen35_batch.rs`); a batch containing a full cache fails
and leaves both caches unchanged.

