| long_plus_two | V7 | 6.8 | 14.10 | 0.85 / 12.46 | 5.64 | 11.70 | 32.8 |
| long_plus_two | batched | 7.3 | 13.18 | 0.69 / 11.53 | 5.21 | 12.28 | 32.4 |
| solo_short | V7 | 14.5 | 0.62 | 0.40 / 0.41 | 0.62 | 1.00 | 38.1 |
| solo_short | batched | 15.7 | 0.57 | 0.35 / 0.35 | 0.57 | 1.00 | 35.6 |
| solo_long | V7 | 5.2 | 12.35 | 10.67 / 10.73 | 12.35 | 1.00 | 37.3 |
| solo_long | batched | 5.6 | 11.48 | 9.86 / 9.88 | 11.48 | 1.00 | 39.0 |

long_plus_two, interactive clients only (a, b): V7 TTFT p50 0.47 / max 0.87 s,
completion 1.21 s, long job 14.10 s; batched TTFT p50 0.55 / max 0.70 s,
completion 1.08 s, long job 13.18 s. The per-scenario TTFT p95 column above
mixes in the long job's own 10-12 s first token for long_plus_two.
Run-to-run clock drift is a few percent (reuse-off session pairs were 7% faster
in the batched session than in the V7 session), so solo rows are not a win.

Session checkpoint regression, ctx 512, 3 pairs, batched binary: code pair 3.289 s
(reuse off 4.467 s), turn-2 TTFT 0.450 s, 170 reused; chat pair 3.423 s (off
4.722 s), turn-2 TTFT 0.469 s, 186 reused. Texts equal in every pair.

Peak process VRAM (nvidia-smi, 2 Hz), V7 -> batched: decode3 4632-4664 -> 4792-4856
MiB; long_plus_two 4728-4760 -> 4760-4792; three_interactive 4632-4696 -> 4792-4856.
RSS 279-307 MiB (V7 267-290). A staged copy of one sequence is 84 MiB (delta
state 54 MiB + first KV slab + split-attention scratch), and a batch stages
every member at once, so B = 3 adds about 170 MiB over V7's one staged copy.

Behaviour checks on the batched binary (behave.py, same as V7): concurrent
sessions isolated, same ID in use -> 404, disconnect mid-prefill frees the GPU
(next request 2.4 s later), 3 x 200 + 2 x 408 + extras 503. New: a client that
disconnects 2 s into a three-way batched decode leaves the two survivors with
text identical to the unbatched reference, and the server answers the same
prompt correctly afterwards.

Verification: fmt, check, Clippy -D warnings, line-limit, unsafe clean; full
workspace tests with MARIN_REQUIRE_CUDA=1 (646 passed, 0 failed, 5 ignored,
including the real-model batch and slice tests) and with MARIN_MATMUL_SCALAR=1
(same); pinned HF verify for Qwen3.5-4B Q8 CUDA unchanged (max logit 1.31e-5,
max layer 1.18e-5, normalized max 3.879e-6, relative RMS 3.288e-6).

## Gate evaluation

| Gate | Result |
|---|---|
| B1 bit-identical logits, B = 2, 3 (4 on fixture) | PASS (real model, 12 steps) |
| B2 atomic failure, cancellation, HF unchanged, texts equal | PASS |
| B3 other backends untouched | PASS (kernel only on the Qwen3.5 precise path) |
| P1 aggregate >= 1.30x (B=2), 1.50x (B=3) | PASS: 1.47x (52.3 vs 35.5), 1.77x (63.5 vs 35.8) |
| P2 three_interactive TTFT p95 <= 1.19 s, mean completion <= 1.86 s | PASS: 0.97 s, 1.41 s (sequential 1.35 s); long_plus_two interactive p95 0.70 s, long job 13.2 s |
| P3 solo within +3% | PASS (0.35 s, 11.5 s; no regression) |
| P4 VRAM growth <= 128 MiB at B = 3 | **MISS: +160 to +190 MiB** (device free at peak >= 2.3 GB, slack 512 MiB intact) |
| Short-only completion fix | Not needed as a separate change: lockstep batching removes it (1.86 -> 1.41 s) |

Decision: GO, with the P4 miss recorded. Cause is understood (B simultaneous
staged copies); admission now reserves one staged copy per batch member. A cap
of 2 members, or staging only the DeltaNet state for decode, would bring
growth under the gate and are the cheap follow-ups if memory matters more.

## Next bottleneck

At B = 3 a batched step is 34 ms of which the per-sequence stages (recurrence
about 1.7 ms, attention, row copies, three staged-state copies of 0.6-0.8 ms)
are about half; the projections are now nearly flat in B. Server aggregate
(63.5/s) is below the engine ceiling (87.7/s) because prompts are still
processed one at a time (about 0.33 s each, 1 s of the 6 s run) and each step
pays thread wake-up and sampling. Candidates: stage only DeltaNet state for
decode; a batched recurrence kernel with per-sequence state pointers; overlap
sampling of one step with the next pass.
